WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:03.760
How can you ensure that your data is
stored consistently

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:09.280
and reliably across a distributed system?
My name is Whitney Lee and I'm a Cloud

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:14.880
Developer here at IBM.
etcd is an open source key value data

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:19.119
store
used to manage and store data that help

00:00:19.119 --> 00:00:23.920
keep distributed systems running.
etcd is most well known for being one of

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:28.480
the core components of Kubernetes,
where it stores and manages Kubernetes

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:35.360
state data, configuration data,
and metadata. etcd can be relied upon

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:41.520
to be a single source of truth at any
given point in time.

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:45.600
Today I'm going to go over some of the
features of etcd that allow it to be so

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:48.559
effective in this way.

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:52.719
etcd is fully replicated.

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:59.520
This means that every node in an etcd
cluster

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:07.920
has access to the full data store.
etcd is also reliably consistent.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:15.040
Every data read in an etcd cluster is
going to return the most recent data

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:19.360
right.
Let's talk about how this works. etcd

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:25.119
is built on top of the Raft algorithm
that is used for distributed consensus.

00:01:25.119 --> 00:01:32.000
So, let's make a very simple etcd cluster
of only four nodes. An etcd cluster

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:36.000
always has a leader
and then the other nodes in the cluster

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:40.560
are followers.
It's a key value data store, so in this

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:44.960
case at key one we have the value of
seven.

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:48.720
Let's say a web application comes in

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:55.920
and lets the leader node know at key one
we want to store the value of 17 instead

00:01:55.920 --> 00:02:00.399
of 7.
The leader node does not change its own

00:02:00.399 --> 00:02:04.560
local data store,
instead it forwards that request to each

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:09.360
of the followers.
When a follower changes its local data

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:12.160
store
it returns that to the leader, so the

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:16.400
leader knows.
When our leader node can see that the

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:20.160
majority of the nodes
have been updated to the most current

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:24.720
data that's when the leader will update
its own current data store

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:29.280
and return a successful write to the
client.

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:32.720
Now client doesn't actually have to
concern itself

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:36.239
about which node in the cluster is the
leader. The client can make

00:02:36.239 --> 00:02:40.319
read and write requests to any node in
the cluster.

00:02:40.319 --> 00:02:44.080
So, let's say, this all happens over a
matter of milliseconds,

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:48.640
but let's say that the client makes a
read request to the node that hasn't

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:53.440
updated yet
and says what's the value at key one?

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:58.239
Well this follower node knows it's
a follower node and knows it's not

00:02:58.239 --> 00:03:02.640
authorized to answer the client directly.
So what it's going to do is forward that

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:07.760
request into the leader node
which will then respond the cluster's

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:14.720
current value at key 1 is 17.
And so it will get a response of 17 to

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:23.840
the client.
And that's how etcd is replicated.

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:28.640
So every every node in the cluster
has access to the full data store

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:32.000
and it's consistent every data read is
going to return

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:39.440
the most recent data right.
etcd is also highly available.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:49.200
This means that there's no single point
of failure in the etcd cluster.

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:53.439
It can tolerate gracefully tolerate
network partitions and hardware failure

00:03:53.439 --> 00:03:57.040
too.
So, let's say that our leader node goes

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:00.560
down.
The followers can declare themselves a

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:03.360
candidate,
they'll hold an election where each one

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:07.760
votes based on availability
and a new node will be elected the

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:10.720
leader.
That leader will go on to manage the

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:17.360
replication for the cluster
and the data is unaffected.

00:04:18.639 --> 00:04:22.880
etcd is also fast.

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:28.400
etcd is benchmarked at 10,000 writes per
second.

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:32.000
With that said, etcd does persist data to
disk.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:37.040
So, etcd's performance is tied to your
storage disk speed.

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:45.520
etcd is secure.
etcd uses transport layer security with

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:49.520
optional
SSL client certificate authentication.

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:53.759
etcd stores vital and highly sensitive
configuration data,

00:04:53.759 --> 00:05:01.759
so it's important to keep it protected.
Finally etcd is simple to use.

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:07.840
A web application can read and write
data to etcd uses a

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:12.320
simple http JSON tools.

00:05:12.479 --> 00:05:15.520
So the other thing to talk about in etcd
that's important

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:19.919
is the watch function. Kubernetes
leverages this.

00:05:19.919 --> 00:05:26.720
So, as i talked about at the beginning,
etcd stores Kubernetes configuration data

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:29.759
and its state data.

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:39.440
So, etcd can use this watch function
to compare these to each other. If they

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:42.560
ever go
out of sync, etcd will let the Kubernetes

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:45.600
API know
and the kubernetes API will reconfigure

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:48.720
the cluster accordingly.

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:57.039
etcd can be used to store your data
reliably and consistently across your

00:05:57.039 --> 00:06:01.039
distributed system.
